00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:15.589 align:start position:0%
 
of markets and money  in the interview  nobel economics prize winner alvin roth  professor ross you are karate enthusiast  a high school dropout  a nobel laureate of economics  life-changing experiences seem to be  quite normal for you does it affect your  work  i think everyone has life-changing  experiences that's what life is about  surely it affects my work  but  but i didn't really drop out of high  school i went on and had further  education so i am not  a self-educated man i went to college i  went to graduate school i have a pretty  ordinary education why did you opt for  economics  well i didn't study economics i was  educated as an engineer  and i studied operations research  and operations research when i studied  it was mostly about the kind of  operations that went on in a factory in  a business so one decision maker trying  to organize all his operations but i was  interested in how people interact with  each other and how to coordinate  activity and that led me naturally to  economics how much does economics have  to do with actual with reality i mean is  it just theory or does it also have to  do with reality oh economics has very  much to do with reality uh  you know markets are ancient human  artifacts we've been making markets  probably as long as we've been making  languages which are also human artifacts  and and often we  we think of them as things that we  we can't change that we just have to  accept but market design the kind of  economics that that i've worked on and  many of my colleagues have  is about fixing markets when they're  broken making them work better and  creating markets where they're needed  what kind of markets are broken what do  you mean by that well  the most unusual market that i've worked  on in the united states is what we now  call kidney exchange  in the united states as in most of the  world it's against the law to buy and  sell organs for transplantation but  there's a great need for transplantation  and the available  supply of organs doesn't meet it but for  kidneys  it's  one of the sources of organs is live  donation uh you have two kidneys and if  you're as healthy as i hope you are then  you could give a kidney to someone you  love and save their life but sometimes  you can't give  a kidney to who you love even though  you're healthy enough to give someone a  kidney but your kidney doesn't match the  the person who needs it and i might be  in the same situation and i could give a  kidney to your patient and you could  give a kidney to mine and that's a  kidney exchange that's become a standard  form of transplantation now in the  united states  but it's against the law here in germany  so so germany hasn't  been able to participate in this  increased access to transplantation that  is found in holland and in england and  elsewhere in the eu so you created a  market for kidney well a market a market  is a bigger thing than just involving  money i study matching markets where  where  other things go on there are other  market clearing institutions than just  money so kidneys happen to be a market  in which we forbid the use of money to  decide who gets which kidney but but  nevertheless transactions exchanges can  increase the availability of transplants  if you want to give a kidney to someone  and i want to give a kidney to someone  but we can't but i can give a kidney to  your patient and you can give a kidney  to mine that creates two more  transplants that wouldn't have happened  otherwise through exchange  talking about markets can everything be  put down to markets  well so i use the word market more  broadly than than many people do  often in economics we think of commodity  markets things that that a price is  sufficient but but many markets aren't  like that job markets aren't like that  radio deutsche bella doesn't hire its  broadcasters and interviewers by by  lowering the wage until just enough  people want the job  presumably your job is a very desirable  one that lots of people would like to  have but deutsche bala chose you and and  they couldn't just choose you you had to  choose them you had other opportunities  so so  labor markets employment is not a market  that's said only by the price by the  wage but it has  lots of other features and once you  start thinking of markets that way  many of the ways that we coordinate and  cooperate and compete are markets but do  you see also markets  going wrong like for example financial  markets which seems to be very much  governed by greed the one has the  impression well markets again are human  artifacts and sometimes they work the  way we want and sometimes they don't and  then we should try to fix them so uh  it's certainly true that that markets  have rules that  change and can be changed and can be  regulated and all of those are things we  should always be thinking about  monitoring the markets and see if  they're doing the job that we want them  to do  are you sometimes worried about the  markets what markets are doing sure  that's what market designers do we we  worry about what markets are doing and  see if we can fix them whether they're  working as well as they should so for  instance i think a lot about labor  markets and whether employment  opportunities are being distributed the  way we would like them to be  you know when there's a  an economic crisis there's  one of the painful parts of economic  crisis is unemployment  and a lot of unemployment has to do just  with the  suppression of economic activity with  lack of economic activity but but even  during a recession there are employers  looking for workers who can't find them  and workers qualified for the jobs  looking for work who can't find  jobs and that's a coordination problem  so in various marketplaces  my colleagues and i have helped develop  labor market clearinghouses that help  people find jobs now here in europe many  people are unemployed in countries like  greece or spain so what methods would  you apply to help these kind of people  in the labor market so so i don't know  enough about european economies to to  give a clear answer but  there are there are long term and there  are short-term problems some of the  long-term problems  require years to repair how how to  find the right education for the  workforce how to  uh how to allow people to move around so  the eu now allows lots of mobility but  of course  uh  in the eu there are barriers to mobility  that don't exist in the united states  where where we speak the same language  across the whole continent so it's not  easy to move from greece to germany in  order to find employment  but  easing barriers to transportation making  making a common currency making  finding common languages that people  work in uh all of these will help  increase labor mobility and and that's  something that that over the longer term  has certainly helped the american  economy to to deal with unemployment  finding partners nowadays is also a  topic for many people how do i find the  correct or the right partner can you  could you help some set as well well so  so there are i mean finding the right  partner is a little like finding the  right job these are matching markets  my colleagues and i have mostly worked  on employment trying to to ease  congestion for instance in the market  for new economists or the market for new  doctors so so most new doctors in the  united states get their first jobs  through a clearinghouse that i've helped  to design  but but increasingly we're seeing  internet dating sites which are matching  markets that have rules and experience  some of the same problems of congestion  for example that that labor markets can  experience  and colleagues of mine have have studied  these and sometimes suggested design  interventions that help them work better  when they work better what does it mean  for example well so  dating markets is a good example  one of the things that people who study  dating markets notice is that women who  have attractive pictures often get many  more emails than they can answer and  this  causes men whose emails aren't being  answered to send more emails so there  starts to be lots of congestion on these  sites not so much information is passing  back and forth between all the messages  one of the things we did in the  economics job market is we allowed job  candidates to send a small number of  signals to jobs they were particularly  interested in that helped employers sort  among the many applications they got to  to understand which ones to interview so  i have some colleagues who made the same  kind of intervention on a dating website  they gave people a limited number of  virtual roses they called them and you  could send a message that had a rose and  a message that had a virtual rose  attached was a little bit of a special  message because you only had very few  roses so those messages got more  attention and made it easier to cut  through the congestion  are there any markets which you would  like to improve or you look at and think  i could apply my theories to these  markets i could improve them well i keep  thinking about  transplantation i'm doing a great deal  of work on that and have been for some  years my colleagues and i have also done  a lot of work on assigning children to  schools in american cities  that's  an important problem  and we're learning how to do it better  and we've we've helped design school  choice  systems in new york city and in boston  and some other big american cities  and we're hoping to learn how to do that  better  certainly you brought up financial  markets  a student of mine named eric buddish has  been thinking a lot lately about how  computerized trading has  changed the need for market rules and  how rules of the market that worked well  when human beings were doing all the  trading may not work as well at the high  speeds at which computers trade so i  think that we're probably going to see  some  changes in the rules of financial  markets how transactions are made that  will help them work better from an  economic stand standpoint are we living  in a stable world or are you worried  about the economy  well those aren't the only possibilities  i think we're not living in a stable  world things are changing all the time  but i'm not terribly worried we  have some crises but uh but by and large  prosperity has been improving over  the last century uh a great deal it's  it's i think few people even if they're  worried about present times would prefer  to live a hundred years ago than today  and so i think the long-term trend is  that we  encounter crises but we we learn to  overcome them we  there's a changing economic environment  but by and large it  helps us live our lives better so i'm  optimistic  and what does it mean for your science  for the economic science does it have to  change  as well absolutely economics is a young  science first of all we don't know very  much  uh  but but you know we're learning about it  and um  some of the things we're learning are  not the things that you often read about  in the newspaper but but more  engineering kinds of things we're  learning how to make things work better  the environment changes the challenges  change and we learn more we're a very  early science it's an exciting thing to  study it's an important thing to study  economics is about what people do  but but there are there are still big  limits to what we understand about that  thank you very much for that interview  you're quite welcome thank you

